 # This class is part of an EXPERIMENTAL API.
class JobService:
    """JobService makes, reads, cancels and retries a tenant's jobs. A job is a
    piece of work a person or a rule asks for, kept in the tenant's database
    until it ends, so it survives a restart of the server midway and goes on
    from where it was. A job has a kind: send, the archived instances of a
    subject, a session or a scan sent to one of the tenant's senders
    (SenderService); or deid, the archived instances of a subject, a session,
    a scan or the whole tenant read and judged against a de-identification
    profile version, changing nothing (DeidJob); scrub, every stored object of
    the tenant read again and compared with what the catalog and the scan's
    manifest say it should be (ScrubJob); or rebuild, the catalog made again
    from what the tenant's storages hold (RebuildJob); or import, the files of
    a directory under one of the installation's roots handed to an import
    receiver (ImportJob). A kind added later is a new member of Job's kind,
    served by these same methods.

    What a send job sends. The instances the catalog held for its target when
    the job was made: each instance archived at or before the job's
    create_time. An instance archived later into the same session is sent by
    its own forward or by a new job, never by this one. An archive that began
    before create_time and ended after the job listed its scan is the job's
    all the same: it is sent, and the counts grow by it when it is. A job
    made by a receiver's forward_to (Receiver) sends exactly the instances
    one reception archived, and names that reception and its receiver; the
    server makes one such job per sender the receiver names when the
    reception reaches done, in the same transaction, and never two for the
    same reception and sender, so a step run again makes nothing twice.
    Forwarding never delays a reception or changes how it ends.

    How a send job runs. Its work is one unit per scan of its target, each
    sending the scan's instances in the catalog's order on one association
    (for a stow sender, a series of requests). A unit records how far the
    peer has answered, in batches of at most 64 instances or one second; a
    unit taken over after a crash or a lost lease goes on after the last
    instance recorded. So a send is at least once: an instance the job counts
    as sent was answered by the peer, and after a crash at most the instances
    answered since the last record, one batch per unit, are sent again. An
    instance a stow peer leaves unaccounted does not hold the record back:
    the unit moves past it and keeps it owed, tries it once more by name, and
    only then refuses it as JOB_FAILURE_CODE_PEER_UNACCOUNTED; a crash in
    between still sends at most one batch again. Every peer seen treats the
    same SOP Instance UID with the same bytes as a repeat; a peer that keeps
    both copies would keep both.

    What the peer's answer means, for each instance. A DIMSE sender reads the
    C-STORE response's status (PS3.4 B.2.3, PS3.7 Annex C): 0000 is sent;
    B000, B006 and B007 (coerced, elements discarded, data set does not match
    its SOP class) are sent with a warning, counted apart, because the peer
    changed what it keeps; A7xx (out of resources) is a temporary failure of
    the unit, which keeps its place and is tried again later; any other
    status, A9xx, Cxxx, 0122 (SOP class not supported) and 0124 (not
    authorized) among them, refuses the instance, which is listed with its
    status (ListJobFailures), and the unit goes on. A stow sender reads the
    answer as StowSender says. A connection that fails, an association that
    is aborted, a timeout, or a stow request answered 408, 429 or a 5xx, is a
    temporary failure of the unit.

    Attempts. A unit that fails for a temporary cause is tried again after a
    wait that doubles from 10 seconds to an hour; it has 30 attempts, about a
    day at the longest wait. While its sender is down or disabled, no attempt
    is made and none is used, so a peer down overnight does not fail a job. A
    unit whose attempts run out ends with its instances not sent, and its job
    ends failed once its other units end. When the unit that lists the job's
    scans runs out, the job ends failed at once with its units that have not
    started, and a unit still sending records nothing more: a job's counts
    are final once it ends failed. RetryJob sends what was not sent; it may
    send again at most one batch of each unit that was still sending.

    How a job ends. Done: every instance sent, with or without a warning.
    Partial: some refused, every other one sent. Failed: some instances not
    sent because attempts ran out. Canceled: CancelJob, or the removal of
    its sender with cancel_jobs. Each state is reached in one transaction
    with its event (EVENT_TYPE_JOB_STEP); while a job runs, its counts are
    reported at most once every 10 seconds (EVENT_TYPE_JOB_PROGRESS). Events
    carry ids, labels, counts and the peer's status codes, never a UID or a
    value read from a file.

    How much is kept of each job's failures is the setting job.failures-kept
    (SettingsService): the refused instances, and those sent with a warning,
    a job lists, from 100 to 1000000; an ordinary setting, whose
    installation value the installation's admin sets and a tenant's admin
    may override for the tenant; built-in default "10000". Past it, each is
    still counted, in the job's counts and failures_not_listed, and not
    listed.

    What a deid job does. Its work is the same as a send job's: a listing
    unit, then one unit per scan, each reading the scan's instances in the
    catalog's order after its cursor, every byte checked against the length
    and SHA-256 the catalog holds as a send reads it, and recording how far
    it got in batches of at most 64 instances or one second; a cancel takes
    effect within one batch, a crash judges again at most one batch per unit,
    and RetryJob judges again what was refused or never judged. Its counts
    read: sent, the instances that passed; warned, those that passed with a
    note (JobFailureCode says which); refused, those that did not pass. A
    deid job's attempts, states and events are a send job's, and its
    failures are listed by ListJobFailures. It changes nothing: no catalog
    row, no stored byte, no lookup table, no reception. A deid job never
    waits for a sender; the server runs at most 2 of its units at once, and
    at most 1 of one tenant's, each holding one file of at most 64 MiB in
    memory with its de-identified copy (a larger file is not judged and
    passes with the note JOB_FAILURE_CODE_TOO_LARGE), and its reads count in
    the server's bound on reads of archived bytes. Its counts per rule and per reason are answered
    by GetJob (Job.tallies): names of rules, actions, reasons, causes,
    element tags and SOP classes, never a value read from a file. The modes:

    A rule's conditions read the instance's reception as kept: its receiver,
    its calling AE title when its files came with exactly one, and the
    called AE title of its receiver as configured now. A condition on an
    element of the file (Modality, SOP Class UID, Manufacturer, Model Name,
    Software Versions) reads the archived file, which says what the file
    said only where the version that archived it kept the element.

    - A dry run (DEID_JOB_MODE_DRY_RUN) runs a profile version over each
    archived file in memory, as a trial run does (ProfileService
    TryProfile): the engine under keys made for the job and never the
    tenant's, its lookups answering every value unassigned, then the
    check after; it answers what the version would archive and what it
    would hold and why. It judges de-identification alone: no
    receiver's checks run. An image a pixel rule decodes is bounded by
    the lower of the tenant's deid.pixel-limit and 256 MiB.
    - A check (DEID_JOB_MODE_CHECK) verifies each archived file against
    the profile version the catalog records for it, by what that version
    would leave of each element of the archived file: no element it
    removes (a private element it does not keep among them); no value in
    an element it empties; for an element it removes or empties unless
    the IOD requires it, nothing but what the IOD keeps (the element as
    it is, or the version's dummy); Patient Identity Removed (0012,0062)
    YES; De-identification Method (0012,0063) naming the version; and no
    UID outside the standard's registry that is a Study, Series or SOP
    Instance UID the catalog holds for an archived file as received, or
    the tenant's keyed remapping of one it holds de-identified (a lookup
    that answers only whether it was found; a UID the remapping itself
    made, as in de-identified data received again, is no finding). Only
    those UIDs are recognized: an original Frame of Reference UID, or a
    Referenced UID of an instance never archived, is not. An element
    whose decision rests on a condition the archive cannot answer (a
    fact of the reception not known, or an element the version removed
    or changed) is no finding: the file passes with the note
    JOB_FAILURE_CODE_UNDECIDED, tallied check|undecidable|<tag>. An
    instance archived as received records no version and is passed with
    a note (JOB_FAILURE_CODE_NOT_DEIDENTIFIED).

    What a scrub job does. A storage is the archive's truth, and a byte can
    change on it without anyone writing it (a failing disk, an operator's
    mistake, a bucket edited by hand); a scrub finds such a change before a
    reader does. Its work is a unit per scan, which reads each instance of
    the scan in full and compares its length and SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) with
    the catalog's, and the manifest's SHA-256 with the one the catalog holds
    and its lines with the catalog's rows; and units that list the storage's
    keys one level at a time, for objects no scan names. A scan with a
    correction (CorrectionService) not yet purged is checked against its
    correction record, so what the correction is doing (a new revision, a
    relabel's new tree, objects waiting for their purge) is never called an
    orphan or damaged. An object the storage answers as not online (held in
    a tier it cannot read at once) is tallied "unreadable|not_online", never
    a finding and never a mark: not readable now is not missing. Every
    difference is read a second time before it is
    believed, then recorded as a finding (ListScrubFindings) of one of five
    kinds (ScrubFindingKind), with an EVENT_TYPE_SCRUB_FINDING event; a
    damaged or missing instance is marked so that every read of it answers
    data loss at once instead of streaming bytes that are not what was
    archived. A finding read good by a later scrub is cleared, and the
    clearing is recorded. A scrub reports and marks only: it never deletes,
    moves, rewrites or repairs a stored object. Repair is a replace from a
    file sent again (CorrectionService.Replace). It reads at most the
    tenant's setting scrub.rate per storage (bytes per second, such as
    "64MiB"; built-in default "64MiB"), and its reads count in the server's
    bound on reads of archived bytes, behind reception. It also verifies the
    tenant's audit log (AuditSealService.VerifyAudit), and a log that does
    not verify is a tally of the job. The server makes one by itself every
    scrub.interval (a period such as "30d", from "1d" to "3650d", or "0" for
    never; a tenant setting; built-in default "30d"), and a person makes one
    with CreateJob. Its counts read: sent, the instances read good; refused,
    those with a finding; and its tallies, "finding|<kind>" for the findings
    recorded, "cleared" for those cleared, "orphan_objects" for the objects
    no scan names, "unreadable|not_online" for the objects not online, and
    "audit|not_verified" when the log did not verify.

    What a rebuild job does. It reads the correction records on each of the
    tenant's storages first, then lists their "meta/" keys and reads each
    scan's manifest and metadata, and opens no DICOM file. In both modes it
    builds a shadow catalog, in a scratch database of the tenant's database
    type, scan by scan, each write fenced by its unit's attempt, so a crash
    or a lost lease goes on from the last scan written to the shadow
    (RetryJob likewise) and the catalog in use is never touched while it is
    built: it keeps answering reads and sends. A scan under a committed
    delete is left out and tallied; a correction record whose row the
    tenant's database lacks is applied fail-closed (a delete as committed)
    and tallied, and the correction listed as unresolved for an admin; a
    scan whose metadata names a profile version the tenant does not have is
    left out and tallied, never cataloged as archived as received; two
    storages holding one Series Instance UID is a tally, not a choice the
    job makes, except a relabel's two trees, which its record resolves. In
    compare mode it tallies each difference from the catalog in use and
    drops the shadow. In replace mode the tenant's receivers refuse new data
    while the shadow is built (a transient refusal the sender tries again,
    as when a storage is nearly full), so nothing lands that the shadow
    would miss, and corrections are refused; once the build is complete
    (every key listed, every manifest read, the counts adding up) one
    transaction makes the shadow the catalog in use, with an
    EVENT_TYPE_CATALOG_REBUILT event, so every read sees the whole old
    catalog or the whole new one, never part of either. Its counts read:
    sent, the instances cataloged (or compared); refused, those left out.
    Its tallies: "differs|<table>|<added|removed|changed>",
    "left_out|profile_unknown", "left_out|deleted",
    "unresolved_correction", "series_on_two_storages", and "activated" once
    a replace made its shadow the catalog in use.

    What an import job does. It reads the directory its path names under its
    receiver's root (ImportReceiver) and hands each DICOM file in it to the
    receiver, one at a time, as a file that arrived; it never writes, moves
    or deletes anything in the root. For example, a tree kept as
    <study>/<session>/series/<scan>/<file>, with a map from (study, session)
    to subject, is read by an import receiver with the pattern layout
    "^(?P<study>[^/]+)/(?P<session>[^/]+)/series/(?P<scan>[^/]+)/[^/]+$",
    unit depth 2 (one unit per session folder), map_key_columns naming the
    map's study and session columns, map_key_parts ["study", "session"],
    map_value_column its subject column and map_gives "subject", and the
    naming rule "{study}_{subject}", "{study}_{session}",
    "{scan}". Its work is a listing unit and one unit
    per folder. The listing unit lists the folders at the layout's unit depth
    under the job's path, a page at a time, and queues one unit per folder
    (one unit for a path at or below that depth); it also judges every file
    it meets above that depth (skipped as outside the layout, not DICOM and
    so on, or handed in when the layout matches it), and ListImportUnits
    shows it as a unit of its own, its folder the job's path. Each unit walks
    its folder one directory at a time, in the order of its paths compared
    segment by segment, each segment byte by byte, so a directory's whole
    subtree comes before the next name of that directory ("a/b/c" before
    "a/b.d", which plain byte order of whole paths would reverse, "/" being
    below "."); its cursor is a path in that order. A directory is read as
    names only, at most the installation's setting import.max-dir-entries of
    them (a whole number from 1000 to 10000000; installation-only, since it
    bounds the server's memory; built-in default "200000", about 20 MiB of
    names): the bound is on reading one directory, not on the walk, which
    holds one directory's names at a time. A directory with more is not read
    further and is skipped whole as IMPORT_SKIP_CODE_DIRECTORY_TOO_WIDE, so
    raising the setting (it applies at the next directory read, no restart)
    and retrying imports it. Each path segment is opened without following a
    link (no-follow open, the type read without following, as lstat does), so
    a link, wherever it is in the tree, is skipped as
    IMPORT_SKIP_CODE_NOT_REGULAR and nothing outside the root is ever opened.
    Before a file is handed in, its first 132 bytes are read: one without the
    Part 10 preamble and "DICM" (PS3.10 7.1) is skipped as
    IMPORT_SKIP_CODE_NOT_PART10 and never reaches quarantine, so a tree's
    notes, images, archives and index files do not fill it.

    A unit writes each file's record (its arrival, or the row of its skip)
    in the same transaction as its cursor and its counts found, skipped and
    handed_in, fenced by its attempt. After a crash or a lost lease it goes
    on after its cursor, and no file is handed in twice, so handed_again is
    always 0 in this version; it is kept for a later version that batches
    these writes. The copy a hand-off lands in the incoming area is named by
    the arrival's id, never by its path, and the arrival's record carries
    the path, in the tenant's database. A unit stops with its place kept,
    using no attempt, while the receiver is disabled, the archive cannot
    take files (the incoming area or the storage below its reserve, the
    tenant at its incoming.limit or not ready), the tenant is restoring,
    moved or fenced, or a rebuild that replaces the catalog runs (whose
    receivers refuse new data); a tenant restored from a backup resumes each
    unit from the cursor the backup holds. The tenant runs at most its
    setting import.max-units of import units at once (a whole number from 1
    to 64; set on a tenant by the installation's admin only, whose
    installation value is the default for every tenant; built-in default
    "4"), and the server at most import.server-max-units of every tenant's
    together (a whole number from 1 to 256; installation-only; built-in
    default "8"), so an import never takes the pipeline from live receivers.
    A unit whose root no longer resolves to the directory it resolved to
    when the job was made (a link in its path changed), or cannot be opened,
    ends failed at once with the reason "root unavailable"; that is not a
    skip of any file.

    An import is done when every file it found has an outcome. A hand-off
    returns once the file is in the server's incoming area, before it is
    identified, de-identified, archived or quarantined; each file handed in
    carries the job and its unit as its arrival's source, kept with the
    received file and with any reception RetryReception makes for it, and
    the pipeline adds its outcome to the job's counts in the transaction
    that decides it: archived, already held, quarantined (by reason), lost,
    failed (its reception failed) or discarded (its reception discarded,
    ReceptionService.DiscardReception). RetryReception moves a failed file
    back to processing; a quarantined file later released keeps its first
    outcome, quarantined, and the release is the quarantine's record, not
    the job's. So the counts reconcile, per job and per unit (ImportCounts,
    ListImportUnits): found plus handed again equals skipped plus handed in,
    and handed in equals archived plus already held plus quarantined plus
    lost plus failed plus discarded plus processing.

    The archive as it is now decides a file's outcome. A file whose SOP
    Instance UID the tenant holds and whose received checksum equals that of
    any revision the instance has had counts already held (tallied
    "superseded_revision" when it is not the current one), so a tree read
    again after a correction does not bring an old version back. A file of a
    target deleted from the archive (CorrectionService.Delete) is
    quarantined as QUARANTINE_REASON_WAS_DELETED, as from any receiver, and
    makes the job partial. A route condition on an AE title never holds for
    a file an import hands in, since none came by association.

    After its last unit's walk the job stays running, with the reason
    "settling", until processing is zero, and only then ends. A job whose
    files are still in a reception an hour past the receiver's max_open_time
    after the last walk ended ends partial, with the reason "settling timed
    out" and the files left in processing counted. Its counts keep moving
    after it ended until processing is zero, and EVENT_TYPE_JOB_PROGRESS
    events go on for it until then; its state does not change. Its states:
    done, every file found has an outcome and none was quarantined, lost,
    failed or discarded, or skipped as unreadable, too large or as a
    directory too wide (files skipped by design, not DICOM, outside the
    layout, of an unmapped session, or not regular files,
    leave it done); partial, otherwise; failed, a unit's attempts ran out or
    its root was unavailable, with what it had handed in still counted and
    still settling; canceled, by CancelJob, which withdraws nothing already
    handed in: those files go on to their outcome and are counted. Each file
    skipped, quarantined, lost, failed or discarded is
    listed, with its path, to an operator by ListImportFiles, up to
    job.failures-kept. A job made again over the same tree finds the files
    added since as new and the others as already held. Events carry counts
    only (JobCounts), never a path; the audit records the receiver's root by
    name and the job's id.

    A path can hold what identifies a person (a folder named after a
    patient), so a path is kept only in the tenant's database (a unit's
    cursor and folder, a skipped file's row, an arrival's record, a
    quarantined file's record), answered only to an operator or more on the
    tenant, and never written to a log, an event or an audit entry; and so
    is a map, inline or read from a file.

    A job meets the archive as it is now, before each instance: an instance
    deleted since the job was made is counted refused
    (JOB_FAILURE_CODE_DELETED_IN_ARCHIVE), one marked damaged fails as data
    loss, and a replaced one is sent or read at its current revision, which
    its outcome records. An instance whose transfer had started when a
    correction committed finishes, since a store cannot be taken back, and
    is tallied "superseded_during_send". A job belongs to the tenant, not to
    the key that made it: revoking that key does not stop it; CancelJob
    does. While the tenant is restoring, moved or fenced, its units stop
    with their place kept, and the job's reason says "tenant restoring",
    "tenant moved" or "tenant fenced".

    Every method acts inside one tenant, named by the request's tenant field,
    so a request into a tenant that is not ready is answered UNAVAILABLE. No
    answer of this service speaks of another tenant.
    """

